><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gorani-language"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gorani-language.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gorani language</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Gorani is a Northwestern Iranian language spoken in parts of the Zagros on both sides of the Iran–Iraq border, closely related to Zazaki.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gorazd-of-carantania"><span class="enc-name">Gorazd of Carantania</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gorazd was a prince of Carantania, the son of Borut, who succeeded his father around 750.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gordian-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gordian-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gordian I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gordian I was a Roman emperor in 238.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gordian-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gordian-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gordian II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gordian II was Roman emperor for about three weeks in 238, jointly with his father Gordian I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gordian-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gordian-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gordian III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gordian III was Roman emperor from 238 to 244, raised to the throne at thirteen after the deaths of his grandfather Gordian I, his uncle Gordian II and the senatorial…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gordion"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gordion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gordion</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gordion was the capital of ancient Phrygia, on the Sakarya River west of modern Ankara in Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gordon-guggisberg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gordon-guggisberg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gordon Guggisberg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sir Gordon Guggisberg was a Canadian-born British army engineer who was governor of the Gold Coast from 1919 to 1927.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gorey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gorey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gorey</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gorey is a harbour village on the east coast of Jersey, in the parish of Grouville and St Martin, facing the coast of Normandy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gorgan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gorgan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gorgan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gorgan, historically Gurgan or Jurjan, is a city and region on the south-eastern shore of the Caspian Sea in northern Iran, the ancient Hyrcania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gorgias"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gorgias.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gorgias</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gorgias was a Greek sophist and orator from Leontini in Sicily.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gorgippia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gorgippia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" 