h="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gortyn Code</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gortyn Code is the longest surviving ancient Greek law code, inscribed in the Doric dialect of Crete on a curved stone wall at Gortyn around 450 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gorumahisani"><span class="enc-name">Gorumahisani</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gorumahisani is an iron-ore hill in Mayurbhanj district, northern Odisha, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/goryeong"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/goryeong.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Goryeong</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Goryeong is a county in North Gyeongsang Province, South Korea, west of Daegu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/goryeosa"><span class="enc-name">Goryeosa</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Goryeosa is the official history of the Korean kingdom of Goryeo, compiled under the Joseon dynasty by scholars including Jeong In-ji and completed in 1451.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/goryeo-khitan-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/goryeo-khitan-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Goryeo–Khitan War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 993–1019</span><span class="enc-snip">The Goryeo–Khitan War was a series of invasions of the Korean kingdom of Goryeo by the Khitan Liao dynasty between 993 and 1019.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/goryokaku"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/goryokaku.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Goryōkaku</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Goryōkaku is a star-shaped Western-style fortress in Hakodate, Hokkaido, completed by the Tokugawa shogunate in 1864.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gorze-abbey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gorze-abbey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gorze Abbey</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Gorze Abbey was a Benedictine monastery near Metz in Lorraine, founded in 757 by Bishop Chrodegang of Metz.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gosamaru"><span class="enc-name">Gosamaru</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gosamaru was a Ryukyuan lord and military commander of the fifteenth century, known for building the castle of Zakimi and strengthening Nakagusuku.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gosanke"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gosanke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gosanke</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gosanke, the 'three honourable houses', were the three senior branch families of the Tokugawa, founded by sons of Tokugawa Ieyasu: the Owari, Kii and Mito houses.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/goseong-county-south-gyeongsang"><span class="enc-name">Goseong County, South Gyeongsang</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Goseong is a county on the south coast of South Gyeongsang Province, South Korea, facing a deeply indented bay.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gospel of John</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gospel of John is the fourth of the canonical gospels of the New Testament, probably written around 90–110 CE.</span></a>